HB.add({
  id: "04",
  intro: `Parsing turns files into clean text <b>with structure</b> — headings, reading order, lists, tables, page numbers — and handles special content such as scans, images, charts, slides and recordings. Chunking, citations and search all depend on this structure, and most real RAG quality problems (garbled tables, mixed columns, missing scanned pages) start here, long before any model is involved.`,
  groups: [
    { id: "4.1", title: "Text Extraction", items: [
      { t: `Native vs Scanned PDFs`,
        s: `A native PDF contains real text characters; a scanned PDF contains only pictures of pages, with no text until OCR reads it.`,
        h: `Check each page separately: extract its text layer and count the characters. A page with plenty of text is native — read it directly, which is fast and exact. A page with almost no text but large images is scanned and needs OCR. Many PDFs are mixed: a native report with a scanned signature page, or a scan that already has a low-quality OCR layer added by the scanner. Decide per page, not per file, and record the decision so you know which text came from OCR.`,
        u: `Apply this check to every PDF page during ingestion. It is especially important for archives (old contracts, invoices, HR files), documents received by email or fax, and mixed packages such as signed agreements. Store a per-page "ocr_used" flag and OCR confidence as metadata, so quality issues can be traced and low-confidence pages reviewed. Re-run the check when you upgrade OCR engines, because previously unreadable pages may now be recoverable.`,
        p: `If scanned pages are treated as native, they produce empty chunks and the document silently becomes unanswerable — users conclude the system "doesn't know" something that is in the archive. If every page is sent to OCR, processing becomes 10–100 times slower and native text gets worse, because OCR introduces errors that the original text layer did not have. Per-page detection gives the best of both: exact text where it exists, OCR only where it is needed.`,
        e: `A 40-page contract arrives: pages 1–38 have a normal text layer and are read directly in under a second; pages 39–40 (signed annexes) contain only images, so they go to Tesseract at 300 DPI. The chunks from those two pages are tagged as OCR text with their confidence, and a question about the annex's termination date is answered correctly with a citation to page 39. Without the per-page check, those two annex pages would have produced empty chunks and the termination date would have been unanswerable.`,
        c: `
import fitz  # PyMuPDF
for page in fitz.open("contract.pdf"):
    chars = len(page.get_text().strip())
    kind = "native" if chars >= 50 else "scanned -> OCR"
    print(page.number + 1, chars, kind)` },
      { t: `OCR`,
        s: `Optical Character Recognition: reading text from images of pages, photos or screenshots.`,
        h: `The page is rendered as an image at about 300 DPI, cleaned (grayscale, binarisation, deskewing, noise removal), and text regions are detected. A neural recogniser — typically a CNN plus an LSTM or Transformer — converts each region into characters with a confidence score and position. Finally the words are assembled into lines and paragraphs. Engines include Tesseract (free, CPU), EasyOCR and PaddleOCR (deep learning, better on photos), cloud OCR services, and vision-language models for very difficult pages.`,
        u: `Use OCR for scanned PDFs, photographed documents, faxes, screenshots in guides, image-only slides and forms. Install the language packs your documents need, render at sufficient resolution, and keep confidence scores. For forms and tables, consider engines with layout and table support. Measure quality on a sample of your own documents rather than trusting benchmarks, because scan quality varies enormously between sources.`,
        p: `Large archives — invoices, old contracts, signed forms, legacy manuals — exist only as images. Without OCR they are invisible to search, and the RAG system cannot answer anything about them. OCR makes them searchable, but it also introduces errors, especially in numbers, so its output must be treated with more care than native text: confidence tracking, review of low-quality pages, and citations that let users check the original image.`,
        e: `A scanned 2009 lease is OCR'd at 300 DPI with Tesseract. The question "When does the lease end?" is answered "31 December 2027 [1]", citing page 3. Because one digit had low confidence, the system shows a "from scanned text" note on the citation, and the user opens page 3 to confirm the date before acting on it. Pages whose overall confidence is low are queued for re-scanning, so the archive gets better over time instead of silently hiding bad text.`,
        c: `
import fitz, pytesseract
from PIL import Image
page = fitz.open("scan.pdf")[0]
pix = page.get_pixmap(dpi=300)
img = Image.frombytes("RGB", (pix.width, pix.height), pix.samples)
data = pytesseract.image_to_data(img, lang="eng", output_type=pytesseract.Output.DICT)
words = [w for w, c in zip(data["text"], data["conf"]) if w.strip() and float(c) > 60]`,
        n: `Numbers are the danger: 0↔O, 1↔l and lost decimal points produce confident wrong answers. Keep confidence scores and let users see the original page.` },
      { t: `HTML Boilerplate Removal`,
        s: `Removing menus, headers, footers, cookie banners, sidebars and ads from web pages, keeping only the main content.`,
        h: `Boilerplate-removal tools such as trafilatura, readability and boilerpy score page blocks by text density, link density and position: navigation has many links and little text, articles have long paragraphs. They return the main article with its headings and lists. For known sites, CSS selectors (<code>article</code>, <code>main</code>, a content div) are even more reliable. Keep the page title and headings, because they become section paths for chunks.`,
        u: `Use it for every crawled or exported web page: help centres, documentation sites, intranet pages, blogs and news. Check results on a sample from each site template, because unusual layouts can make extractors drop real content (for example short FAQ answers) or keep junk (for example "related articles" lists). Keep the canonical URL and last-modified date of each page as metadata, so citations link to the live page.`,
        p: `Without boilerplate removal, every chunk starts with "Home | Products | Support | Login | Accept cookies…". All pages then look similar to the embedding model, keyword search matches navigation words everywhere, and the LLM receives noise instead of content. Clean extraction makes chunks distinct, smaller and much more relevant. It also cuts cost, because embedding and storing navigation text for thousands of pages adds nothing but noise. Cleaner chunks also make reranking more accurate, because the reranker reads actual content instead of menus.`,
        e: `A help-centre page of 3,000 words of HTML (menus, footer, cookie banner, related links) becomes the 420-word article plus its title and two subheadings. After cleaning, a search for "reset router" returns the reset article first instead of every page whose menu contains "Router settings". The chunk count for the site dropped by about a third, and both search speed and answer precision improved. Spot checks on each page template confirmed that no real content had been removed.` },
      { t: `Header / Footer Removal`,
        s: `Deleting text that repeats on every page of a document — company name, "Confidential", page numbers, dates.`,
        h: `Look at the top and bottom bands of each page: lines that appear on most pages (same or nearly the same text, often with a changing page number) are headers or footers. Remove them before building paragraphs. Layout models also label page-header and page-footer blocks directly. Be careful not to remove repeated content that is real, such as a table header repeated on every page. Comparing lines after replacing digits with a placeholder catches footers that differ only in the page number.`,
        u: `Apply it to reports, policies, contracts, manuals and any multi-page PDF or DOCX converted to PDF. It matters most for long documents, where the same footer appears hundreds of times. Keep the page number itself as metadata (for citations), even though the footer text is removed from the content. Check a few pages of each new document template, because some templates put useful information (document number, version) in the header, which should be kept as metadata rather than thrown away.`,
        p: `Repeated headers and footers land in the middle of paragraphs and chunks, splitting sentences ("…the employee must — ACME Corp Confidential Page 7 — notify the manager…"). They add noise to every embedding and make keyword search match words like "confidential" everywhere. Removing them gives cleaner chunks and more precise retrieval. They also distort statistics used later, such as term frequencies in BM25, because a word repeated on every page looks important in that document.`,
        e: `Removing "ACME Corp — Confidential — Page N" from a 200-page manual cleaned 200 chunks. A search for "confidential information policy" previously returned random pages (all contained "Confidential" in the footer); afterwards it returned the actual confidentiality section first. The document version printed in the header was kept as metadata, so citations still show which version each answer came from.`,
        c: `
from collections import Counter
def strip_repeated(pages, min_share=0.6):
    edges = Counter(l for p in pages for l in set(p.splitlines()[:2] + p.splitlines()[-2:]))
    junk = {l for l, n in edges.items() if n >= min_share * len(pages)}
    return ["\\n".join(l for l in p.splitlines() if l not in junk) for p in pages]` },
      { t: `Unicode Normalization`,
        s: `Converting characters that look the same into one standard form, so text extracted from different sources matches.`,
        h: `Unicode can represent the same visible text in several ways: ligatures ("ﬁ" as one character), full-width letters (Ａ), composed vs decomposed accents (é as one character or e + accent), non-breaking spaces and invisible control characters. NFKC normalisation maps compatibility characters to their standard forms. Additional cleaning replaces smart quotes, normalises dashes and whitespace, and removes zero-width and control characters.`,
        u: `Apply normalisation to every document before chunking and indexing, and apply the same normalisation to user queries, so both sides use identical characters. It is especially important for PDFs (which often store ligatures), OCR output, copy-pasted text from different tools, and multilingual content. Run it once at ingestion and keep the normalised text as the canonical version. Do not normalise inside code blocks or exact identifiers where the original characters matter.`,
        p: `Search silently fails when the document stores "ﬁnance" (one ligature character) and the user types "finance", or when a non-breaking space splits a product code. These mismatches are invisible on screen, so nobody understands why an obvious document is not found. Normalisation removes this whole class of problems for almost no cost. It also prevents subtle duplicate problems: two copies of the same paragraph that differ only in invisible characters would otherwise be treated as different chunks.`,
        e: `<code>unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", "ﬁnance ＡＢＣ")</code> returns "finance ABC". After normalising all PDFs, keyword search for "financial statements" started finding policy pages that previously contained the ligature version of "fi", and invisible zero-width characters hidden in a shared document were removed. The same normalisation is applied to queries, so a user typing a full-width digit from a phone keyboard still matches the document. The change required no re-embedding of unaffected documents, because only texts that actually changed got new hashes.`,
        c: `
import unicodedata, re
def clean(text):
    text = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
    text = text.replace("\\u00a0", " ")                       # non-breaking space
    text = re.sub(r"[\\u200b-\\u200d\\ufeff]", "", text)           # zero-width characters
    return re.sub(r"[\\x00-\\x08\\x0b\\x0c\\x0e-\\x1f]", "", text)   # control characters` },
      { t: `Hyphenation & Line-break Repair`,
        s: `Joining words split across lines ("employ-" / "ment") and merging lines that break in the middle of sentences.`,
        h: `PDF text is extracted line by line, as it was laid out on the page. Repair joins patterns of letter–hyphen–newline–letter into one word, merges consecutive lines of the same paragraph into one line, and keeps real paragraph breaks (blank lines), list items and headings. Care is needed with real hyphenated words ("e-mail", "well-known") and code, which should not be altered. A small dictionary check helps decide whether 'well-known' should keep its hyphen.`,
        u: `Apply it to PDF and OCR output, especially justified text in policies, books, academic papers and contracts. Run it after unicode normalisation and header/footer removal, and before chunking, so sentence splitting and chunk boundaries work correctly. Check repaired text on samples from each source, because some documents intentionally use line breaks (addresses, poems, tables) that should not be merged. Keep a switch to disable repair for such sources.`,
        p: `Hyphen-broken words become two meaningless tokens: a search for "employment" misses chunks containing "employ-" and "ment", and embeddings get noisier. Line breaks in the middle of sentences confuse sentence splitters and chunkers, producing awkward chunk boundaries. Repairing them restores normal text for every later step. It also improves embedding quality: models see natural sentences instead of fragments, which places chunks more accurately in vector space.`,
        e: `"The employ-\\nment contract ends\\nafter the notice period." becomes "The employment contract ends after the notice period." After repair, BM25 found the notice-period clause for "employment contract notice", which it had missed before. The repair step runs before chunking, so chunk boundaries now fall at sentence ends instead of in the middle of hyphenated words. A sample review of 50 repaired pages found no damaged words.`,
        c: `
import re
def repair(text):
    text = re.sub(r"(\\w)-\\n(\\w)", r"\\1\\2", text)              # hyphenated word across lines
    return re.sub(r"(?<![.\\n:])\\n(?!\\n|[-•*\\d])", " ", text)    # soft line breaks inside paragraphs` },
      { t: `Language Detection`,
        s: `Identifying the language of each document or passage automatically.`,
        h: `Fast classifiers such as fastText's language-ID model, lingua or langdetect predict the language from character patterns, with a confidence score. Run it per document and, for mixed documents, per section or chunk. Store the result as metadata and use it to choose OCR language packs, text analysers (stemming, stop words), embedding models and the language of the answer. Short texts are harder to classify, so detection is more reliable on paragraphs than on single lines or titles.`,
        u: `Use it in multilingual companies, global support centres, government services and any corpus that mixes languages — for example English policies with Hindi or Tamil sections, or emails in several languages. It also supports reporting (how much content exists per language) and routing questions to language-appropriate indexes. Detect the language of each user question too, and answer in that language. For mixed documents, store a language per chunk.`,
        p: `Applying an English stemmer to German text, or English-only embeddings to Tamil, silently ruins retrieval for those documents — they are indexed but practically unfindable. Language detection makes sure each text gets the right processing, and lets you answer users in their own language. It also helps evaluation: measuring quality per language shows whether some users get much worse answers than others.`,
        e: `A Hindi policy section is tagged lang=hi, processed with a multilingual embedding model and indexed with the right analyser. A user who asks in Hindi gets an answer in Hindi that cites the Hindi section, while English users still find the English version of the same policy. A per-language report showed that Tamil questions had twice the abstention rate of English ones, which led the team to add Tamil versions of the most-asked policies. Those questions were then answered as well as English ones.` },
      { t: `Markdown Conversion`,
        s: `Converting parsed documents into Markdown — headings with #, lists with -, tables with | — as a clean, structured intermediate format.`,
        h: `Modern parsers (Docling, MarkItDown, LlamaParse, Unstructured) can export documents as Markdown. Headings keep their levels, lists stay lists, tables become pipe tables, and emphasis is kept. Markdown is plain text, so it is easy to store, diff and review; it is easy to split by headings; and LLMs read it very well because they saw a lot of Markdown in training. Images are usually replaced by placeholders or captions, and page breaks can be marked so page numbers survive.`,
        u: `Use Markdown as the standard parsed format between parsing and chunking, and store it as an artifact so you can re-chunk without re-parsing. It is also useful for human review of parsing quality ("does this look right?") and for showing source excerpts in the interface. Keep one Markdown artifact per document version, linked to the parser version, so you can compare parser upgrades side by side.`,
        p: `Plain-text extraction loses headings and table structure; raw HTML or JSON parser output is verbose and awkward to chunk. Markdown keeps the structure that structure-aware chunking needs while staying simple. It also makes parsing problems visible to people, which is the fastest way to catch them. It also decouples parsing from chunking: chunking strategies can be changed and tested many times on the same stored Markdown without paying for parsing (and OCR) again.`,
        e: `A DOCX policy becomes "# Leave policy" / "## Annual leave" / "- 24 days per year" / "## Sick leave" … in Markdown. The chunker splits at "##" headings, every chunk gets the section path "Leave policy &gt; Annual leave", and a reviewer can read the Markdown file to confirm the conversion is correct. When chunking was changed in pipeline v2, the stored parsed output was reused, so re-chunking the whole library took minutes instead of hours.`,
        c: `
from docling.document_converter import DocumentConverter
result = DocumentConverter().convert("policy.pdf")
markdown = result.document.export_to_markdown()
print(markdown[:500])` },
      { t: `Email & Attachment Parsing`,
        s: `Extracting the body, headers (from, to, date, subject), thread structure and attachments from emails.`,
        h: `Emails are MIME messages (.eml, or Outlook .msg). The parser picks the plain-text or HTML body, removes quoted replies ("On Mon, X wrote:") and signatures, keeps headers as metadata, and groups messages into threads using Message-ID and In-Reply-To headers. Each attachment is extracted and sent through its own file parser, linked to its parent email, inheriting the email's permissions. Inline images and calendar invitations need their own handling.`,
        u: `Use it for support inboxes, sales and account history, legal discovery, project correspondence and shared mailboxes. Index threads (conversation-level) for context and individual messages or attachments for precise citations, and always apply strict permissions, because mailboxes contain personal data. Apply PII handling to bodies and attachments, and respect the mailbox's access rules — usually only the mailbox members may search it.`,
        p: `Raw email bodies repeat the entire previous conversation in every reply, so the same text is indexed ten times, and signatures and disclaimers add noise to every chunk. Attachments — often where the real answer lives — are ignored by naive parsers. Proper email parsing produces clean conversations and searchable attachments. Emails are also full of personal data and confidential discussions, so parsing must preserve the strict access rules of the original mailbox, not widen them.`,
        e: `A support thread with eight replies becomes one clean conversation document (quoted text removed) plus a linked PDF invoice attachment. A question "what refund did we agree with customer X?" retrieves the reply that states the refund and cites both the email date and the attached credit note. Quoted text removal cut the indexed volume of the mailbox by more than half, and answers stopped citing the same sentence from five different replies.` },
      { t: `Parsing Tools (Unstructured, PyMuPDF, Docling, LlamaParse)`,
        s: `The main libraries and services that turn documents into text and structure.`,
        h: `<b>PyMuPDF</b> is very fast and extracts PDF text with positions and fonts, but has little layout understanding. <b>Docling</b> (IBM, open source) runs a layout model and the TableFormer table model locally and exports Markdown or JSON with reading order. <b>Unstructured</b> handles many formats and returns typed elements, with strategies from fast to high-resolution OCR. <b>LlamaParse</b> is a hosted, LLM-assisted parser for complex PDFs. Cloud document-AI services (Azure, AWS Textract, Google) are strong on forms and tables.`,
        u: `Pick per format and difficulty, and test on your own documents. A common pattern is a fast parser for simple files with a high-quality fallback for complex ones, routed by document type or by a quick quality check. Local tools suit privacy-sensitive data; hosted services suit teams that want the best quality without running models. Keep a small benchmark set of your hardest documents and re-run it whenever you upgrade a parser.`,
        p: `No single tool handles every format and layout well, and choosing by popularity alone leads to bad tables or slow pipelines. Comparing tools on a representative sample of your documents is the only reliable way to choose. Keeping the parser behind an interface lets you switch later without rewriting chunking and indexing. Parsers also change between versions, sometimes for better and sometimes worse, so pinning versions and recording which version parsed each document keeps results reproducible.`,
        e: `This platform uses Docling for PDF, DOCX, PPTX, XLSX, HTML and Markdown (its built-in OCR switched off), PyMuPDF as a fast fallback, plain readers for TXT, CSV and JSON, and Tesseract for scanned pages. A CV PDF parsed by Docling keeps its headings, so every chunk gets a section path like "PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE". Recording the parser version on each output means that when a better Docling version is released, the team can re-parse only the documents processed by the old one.` },
    ]},
    { id: "4.2", title: "Layout & Structure", items: [
      { t: `Layout Analysis`,
        s: `Detecting what each block on a page is — title, heading, paragraph, list, table, figure, caption, header, footer.`,
        h: `A vision model trained on labelled page images (datasets such as DocLayNet and PubLayNet) looks at the rendered page and draws boxes around blocks, labelling each one. The parser then extracts text inside each box and keeps its label. Downstream steps treat each type differently: tables go to table extraction, figures to captioning, headers and footers are removed, headings build the structure. Each block also gets a confidence score, so uncertain regions can be flagged for review.`,
        u: `Use layout analysis for complex PDFs — annual reports, research papers, brochures, forms, slide exports and anything with columns, sidebars or many tables. Simple single-column documents may not need it. Check its output visually on a sample, because layout models can mislabel unusual designs. Running it on GPU is much faster; on CPU it can dominate ingestion time for large PDFs, so plan capacity accordingly.`,
        p: `Without layout analysis, captions, sidebars, footnotes and footers mix into body text, and tables are read as streams of numbers. Chunks become noisy and citations point to confusing text. Layout analysis gives each piece of content its role, which is the basis for everything else in parsing. It also decides what to ignore: logos, decorative images and page furniture can be dropped instead of cluttering chunks.`,
        e: `A financial report page is split into: title, two body paragraphs, a revenue table, a chart and its caption, and a footer. The table is extracted into rows and columns, the chart caption is linked to the chart, and the footer is dropped — so "what was Q3 revenue?" retrieves the table row, not a jumble of numbers. Across the report, layout analysis found 40 tables and 25 charts, which were routed to table extraction and captioning respectively instead of being flattened into text.` },
      { t: `Reading Order Reconstruction`,
        s: `Putting the blocks of a page in the order a human would read them.`,
        h: `PDF files store text in drawing order, which can be arbitrary: the footer first, then column two, then the title. Reading-order reconstruction sorts blocks using layout geometry (columns, top-to-bottom, left-to-right) or a learned model, places figures and sidebars sensibly, and handles elements that span columns, such as titles and wide tables. For forms and slides, order is often defined by position groups rather than columns.`,
        u: `Essential for multi-column documents, newsletters, magazines, academic papers, slides with several text boxes, and forms. It matters most when the order changes the meaning — steps of a procedure, clauses of a contract, conditions and their exceptions. Check it visually: render the extracted text next to the page image for a sample and confirm the order matches what a person would read. Layout-aware parsers like Docling handle most common layouts automatically.`,
        p: `Wrong reading order interleaves unrelated sentences, splits numbered steps, and attaches exceptions to the wrong rules. The LLM then receives text that reads like nonsense or, worse, plausible but wrong combinations. Correct order is invisible when it works, but its absence ruins answers. It also affects chunk boundaries and citations: if text is out of order, a chunk can start in one section and end in another, so its section path and page become wrong.`,
        e: `A two-column research paper is read as: title, abstract, full left column, full right column, then footnotes. Before the fix, the parser read straight across both columns, so every chunk mixed two unrelated paragraphs line by line. After the fix, each chunk contained one coherent paragraph, and a question about the paper's method was answered from the method section instead of a mixture of method and results.` },
      { t: `Multi-column Handling`,
        s: `Correctly reading pages that have two or more columns of text.`,
        h: `Detect column boundaries from gaps in the x-positions of text blocks, read each column top to bottom, then move to the next column. Full-width elements (titles, wide tables, figures) are placed where they appear vertically. Layout models do this automatically; simple heuristics work for regular layouts. Irregular layouts (three columns on one page, two on the next) need per-page detection rather than one rule for the document.`,
        u: `Academic papers, magazines, legal gazettes, government reports, product sheets and newsletters. Test it on each document template you ingest, because column layouts vary and a heuristic tuned for one may fail on another. When documents come from many publishers, a layout model is safer than hand-written heuristics, because each publisher's template is different. Keep a few test pages from each template in a regression set.`,
        p: `Reading straight across columns mixes two paragraphs line by line, producing unreadable chunks and wrong facts in answers. This is one of the most common parsing failures with simple PDF text extraction. The damage is also hard to notice in metrics: retrieval may still find the page, but the text sent to the LLM is scrambled, so answers become vague or wrong.`,
        e: `Sorting PyMuPDF blocks by column (left or right of the page middle) and then by vertical position fixes a two-column brochure: each chunk now contains one coherent paragraph instead of alternating lines from both columns. Questions about the brochure's product features started getting precise answers, because each feature description now stayed in one chunk. The simple rule was later replaced by Docling's layout model to handle three-column pages too.`,
        c: `
import fitz
page = fitz.open("paper.pdf")[0]
mid = page.rect.width / 2
blocks = [b for b in page.get_text("blocks") if b[4].strip()]
ordered = sorted(blocks, key=lambda b: (b[0] >= mid, b[1]))   # left column first, then by y
text = "\\n".join(b[4] for b in ordered)` },
      { t: `Heading Detection`,
        s: `Finding section titles and their level — chapter, section, subsection.`,
        h: `Use the document's own structure when it exists: DOCX "Heading 1/2/3" styles, HTML h1–h6, Markdown #. For PDFs, infer headings from font size and weight, numbering patterns ("3.2", "Article 5"), spacing, and layout-model labels. Assign levels consistently (numbering depth, relative font size) and treat the document title as level 0. Numbered headings give the most reliable levels, because '3.2.1' clearly sits under '3.2'.`,
        u: `Needed for structure-aware chunking, section paths in citations, contextual headers, navigation and summaries per section. It is especially valuable for policies, manuals, contracts, standards and CVs, where headings name the topic of each block. Validate detected headings on samples: too many headings fragment the document, too few merge topics.`,
        p: `Without headings, chunks cut across topics and lose their context: a paragraph saying "this applies to all full-time staff" has no idea which rule "this" is. Headings give every chunk a meaningful label that both search and the LLM can use. Headings also improve BM25 and reranking when indexed as a separate field, because the words in a heading describe the whole section beneath it. They also help users: an answer that cites 'Leave &gt; Annual leave' is easier to trust and verify than one that cites only a page number.`,
        e: `"3.2 Annual leave" (bold, 14 pt, numbered) is detected as a level-2 heading under "3 Leave". Every chunk below it gets the path "Leave policy &gt; Leave &gt; Annual leave", which appears in citations and in the contextual header used for embedding. A question about 'annual leave carry-over' now matches the heading words as well as the text, which ranks the right chunk first.` },
      { t: `Document Structure Tree`,
        s: `A tree of the document: document → sections → subsections → paragraphs, lists and tables.`,
        h: `Built from heading levels: each heading opens a node, and content attaches to the nearest heading above it. Tables, lists and figures become typed child nodes. The tree records page numbers for each node, so any part of it can be cited. Chunkers walk the tree to produce section-based chunks, and metadata (like effective dates) can be inherited down the tree. The tree can be stored as JSON next to the parsed Markdown.`,
        u: `Use it for structure-aware and hierarchical chunking, parent–child retrieval, section summaries, navigation in a document viewer, and to answer "what are the sections of this policy?" questions. It is also a natural basis for document summaries per section and for showing users a table of contents next to cited passages. Keep node IDs stable across re-parses so links do not break. Agents can also use it to navigate long documents section by section instead of reading everything at once.`,
        p: `A flat text stream forgets which section each sentence belongs to, so chunking has to guess boundaries and citations cannot name sections. The tree preserves the author's organisation, which is usually the best map of topics in a document. It also enables metadata inheritance: an effective date or confidentiality label set on a section applies to all of its paragraphs and tables. It is also the input for smarter retrieval patterns such as auto-merging, which replaces many small hits from one section with the section itself.`,
        e: `Handbook → 3 Leave → 3.2 Annual leave → paragraph "24 days per year" (page 4). The chunk created from that paragraph knows its full path and page; a parent-child retriever can also fetch the whole "3.2 Annual leave" node when more context is needed. Section-level summaries generated from the tree let broad questions like 'what does the leave chapter cover?' be answered from one summary instead of many small chunks. When three of its four paragraphs are retrieved for one question, an auto-merging retriever can send the whole section instead, which reads better and wastes fewer tokens.` },
      { t: `List & Bullet Preservation`,
        s: `Keeping list items as separate, ordered items instead of merging them into one run-on sentence.`,
        h: `Detect bullet characters (•, -, *) and numbering (1., a), i.)), keep one item per line with its marker and indentation level, and keep the list together with its introductory sentence when chunking. Nested lists keep their nesting. Markdown output does this naturally. Numbered lists keep their numbers, because users often refer to 'step 3'. Checkbox lists in forms are kept with their checked state, because 'yes/no' matters.`,
        u: `Procedures, requirements, eligibility rules, feature lists, checklists, steps in runbooks and FAQs. These are often exactly what users ask about ("what are the steps to…?"). Keep long lists in one chunk when possible, or split them between items with the introductory sentence repeated. Convert lists to Markdown so the LLM sees clear structure. Lists inside table cells also need care, because flattening them merges items into one line. Keep list markers in Markdown so the LLM reproduces the same structure in its answer.`,
        p: `Lists merged into one line ("Steps: 1 open 2 click 3 save") confuse both the LLM and the user, lose the order of steps, and make it hard to cite a specific step. Preserving lists keeps procedures usable. Split lists are a common chunking failure: the first half of a procedure in one chunk and the second half in another, so the answer gives only half the steps. Lists also carry conditions, such as 'eligible if all of the following apply', where losing one item changes the meaning of the rule.`,
        e: `"To reset your password: 1. Open the portal 2. Click 'Forgot password' 3. Check your email" stays as three numbered lines in one chunk. The assistant answers with the same three steps, in order, citing the guide. A 12-step onboarding procedure is kept as one chunk, so the assistant can list all 12 steps in order. Before, it had split the list into two chunks and answered with only the first seven steps. The same preservation lets the model answer 'what is step 5?' precisely.` },
      { t: `Footnotes & References`,
        s: `Handling footnotes, endnotes and references so they do not interrupt the main text but stay connected to it.`,
        h: `Detect footnote markers (superscript numbers or symbols) in the body and the footnote area (small font at the bottom of the page or end of the document). Remove footnote text from the body flow, then either attach each footnote to the paragraph that references it or store it as its own chunk linked to that paragraph. References and bibliographies are usually stored separately. Endnotes collected at the end of a document are linked back by their numbers. Cross-references inside the text ('see section 4.2') can be kept as links to the target section.`,
        u: `Legal texts, regulations, academic papers, financial reports and policies where exceptions and definitions often live in footnotes. Treat definitions in footnotes as important content, and index them so questions about terms find them. Keep bibliographies separate so they do not pollute search results. Show footnotes in citations when they were used, so users see the exception that changed the answer. In legal documents, treat numbered definitions as first-class chunks.`,
        p: `Footnote text dropped into the middle of a sentence corrupts both; ignoring footnotes loses important exceptions ("except for contractors") and definitions. Linking them correctly keeps the main text readable and the exceptions available. Exceptions in footnotes are a frequent source of wrong answers, because naive parsers drop the small text at the bottom of the page. Cross-references are similar: 'subject to section 7' means the answer may also need section 7, which a smart retriever can fetch.`,
        e: `"Unused leave carries over¹" — footnote 1 says "except for employees on probation". The footnote is attached to that paragraph's chunk, so the answer to "can I carry over leave during probation?" correctly says no, citing the footnote. Without the linked footnote, the answer would have said carry-over is always allowed, which is wrong for probation. The same mechanism links a clause's 'subject to section 7' to section 7, so both are retrieved together.` },
      { t: `Page Numbers & Positions (for citation)`,
        s: `Keeping the page number — and optionally the exact box position — of every piece of extracted text.`,
        h: `Every extracted block records its page and bounding box. Chunks store page_start and page_end (and optionally character offsets). Citations then show "Handbook.pdf, page 4", and a document viewer can open that page and highlight the exact passage using the stored boxes. For DOCX and HTML, which have no fixed pages, use section headings or paragraph anchors instead. Positions are stored in PDF points, so they can be drawn back onto the rendered page at any zoom level.`,
        u: `Citations in every RAG answer, compliance and legal review, audits, and any interface that lets users "show me where it says that". Keep page numbers for OCR'd pages too, so users can open the original scanned image. Store positions even if your viewer does not use them yet; adding highlighting later is then easy. For scanned pages, store the OCR word boxes so highlighting works on images too.`,
        p: `An answer that says only "according to the handbook" cannot be verified quickly in a 200-page document, so users either trust it blindly or ignore it. Page-level citations make verification take seconds and build trust. It also helps compliance and audit, where reviewers must confirm the exact wording behind an answer. Missing positions also make it impossible to show a source preview, which is one of the strongest trust features of a RAG interface.`,
        e: `The answer cites "[1] Employee Handbook.pdf, page 1". Clicking it opens page 1 with the sentence "Every full-time employee receives 24 days of paid annual leave" highlighted, using the bounding boxes stored during parsing. Auditors reviewing answers about expense limits use the highlighted passages to verify them in seconds instead of searching the PDF. The same boxes drive a 'jump to passage' button in the document viewer.` },
      { t: `Section Path Extraction`,
        s: `Recording the chain of headings above a passage, such as "Handbook &gt; Leave &gt; Annual leave".`,
        h: `Walk the structure tree from the root to the passage and join the heading texts with a separator. Store the path on every chunk. Use it in citations, as a boosted keyword field in BM25, and as part of the contextual header that is embedded and reranked together with the chunk text. Long paths can be shortened to the last two or three levels for display.`,
        u: `Every structured document: policies, manuals, contracts, standards, CVs, wikis. It is one of the cheapest and most effective improvements for retrieval quality. Include the document title as the first element of the path. For documents without headings (emails, transcripts), use subject lines, speakers or timestamps as the path. Normalise headings (case, numbering) so paths are consistent across documents of the same template. Show the path in citations and source previews.`,
        p: `A chunk saying "24 days per year" means nothing without knowing it is about annual leave; a CV chunk listing jobs never mentions the person's name. Section paths (with the document title) give every chunk its subject, so it can be found by questions that name that subject. It also makes citations understandable: 'Handbook &gt; Leave &gt; Carry-over, page 4' tells the user immediately whether the source is relevant. It also helps users choose between similar sources, because two chunks with the same words but different sections are clearly distinguished.`,
        e: `Adding the section path and document title to the reranker input lifted a CV's "PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE" chunk from −9.6 to +7.0 for the question "balamurugan experience", because the chunk's own text never contained the person's name. The same path, indexed as a boosted BM25 field, lets typo-tolerant keyword search find the experience section by the person's name. In citations, users see 'CV &gt; PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE' and understand at once which part of the document the answer came from.` },
      { t: `Parsing Quality Validation`,
        s: `Checking that parsing actually worked before the document is indexed.`,
        h: `Compute automatic signals for each document: characters per page, share of non-dictionary words, empty pages, number of headings and tables found, share of OCR pages and their confidence, and repeated-line ratio. Compare them with thresholds per document type; failures go to a review queue. People also review a sample of parsed outputs (as Markdown) for each new document type or parser version. Store the computed signals with each document so trends can be charted over time. Thresholds are set per document type, because a slide deck naturally has fewer characters per page than a policy.`,
        u: `Run validation on every document in production, and run a manual review whenever you add a new source, document type or parser version. Show validation results to document owners, so they can fix bad sources (low-resolution scans, broken exports) themselves. Include parsing checks in the release process for new parser versions: compare old and new outputs on a fixed set of documents before switching.`,
        p: `Parsing fails silently: a 50-page PDF that yields 200 characters is still "processed successfully", indexed, and never answers anything. Validation turns silent failures into visible ones, so they are fixed before users notice. It also protects evaluation: a golden set built on badly parsed documents measures the wrong things. It also builds trust with content owners, who can see that their documents were processed correctly — or exactly why not.`,
        e: `A rule "average fewer than 100 characters per page → failed parse" caught a batch of image-only PDFs whose OCR step had been disabled by mistake. They were re-processed with OCR the same day, instead of being discovered weeks later through user complaints. A weekly chart of failed parses by source made it easy to spot when a connector started exporting documents in a broken format. Owners now receive a short report when one of their uploads fails validation, with the reason and how to fix it.` },
    ]},
    { id: "4.3", title: "Multimodal & Special Content", items: [
      { t: `Table Extraction`,
        s: `Detecting tables and reading them as rows and columns, not as loose words.`,
        h: `A table-structure model (such as TableFormer in Docling, or cloud document AI) finds the grid: rows, columns, header cells and merged cells. The text inside each cell is placed into that structure; for scanned tables, OCR runs per cell. The output is a structured table that can be serialised as Markdown, HTML or JSON. Multi-page tables need their parts joined, and repeated header rows on each page removed. Cell values keep their row and column headers, so each value can be explained in words.`,
        u: `Price lists, financial statements, specifications, schedules, allowance and grade tables, comparison tables in product docs, and any table users ask questions about. Check tables visually on samples: merged cells and multi-row headers are the usual failure points. Store each table with its caption and the sentence that introduces it, because they explain what the table is about. Consider indexing each table twice: as Markdown for the LLM, and as row sentences for semantic search.`,
        p: `Flattened tables lose which number belongs to which row and column, so the LLM confidently pairs the wrong values. Table questions are among the most common failures of naive RAG, and good table extraction is the main fix. Tables also carry many of the numbers users care about — prices, limits, dates — so table errors directly produce wrong numbers in answers, which damages trust quickly. Numeric questions about tables are also a common reason users stop trusting an assistant, because a single wrong amount is easy to spot.`,
        e: `"What is the daily allowance for grade B in Mumbai?" works only if the row "Grade B" and the column "Mumbai" stay connected. With table extraction, the chunk contains a Markdown table and the answer is "₹4,000 per day [1]". Before table extraction, the same question returned '₹2,500', which was the value for grade C in Pune. After the fix, the model could read the correct row and column directly from the Markdown table.` },
      { t: `Table Serialization (Markdown / HTML / JSON)`,
        s: `Writing an extracted table as text in a format that LLMs and search can understand.`,
        h: `Markdown tables (<code>| a | b |</code>) are compact and well understood by LLMs; HTML handles merged cells and nested headers; JSON or "row sentences" ("Grade B, Mumbai: ₹4,000 per day") embed well for semantic search. Large tables are split by groups of rows, with the header row repeated in every piece, plus the table's caption. A short summary of the table can also be indexed separately for broad questions.`,
        u: `Every table that goes into a chunk. Choose Markdown for most tables, HTML for complex headers, and row sentences when users ask about individual rows in natural language. Keep units and footnotes of the table with every piece, because 'amounts in ₹ thousands' changes the meaning of every number. Test which format your LLM reads most accurately on a few real tables. Avoid very wide Markdown tables in small-context models; split wide tables by column groups as well as by rows if needed.`,
        p: `A 200-row table in one chunk exceeds embedding limits and produces a weak vector; splitting it without headers creates rows that mean nothing ("B | 4000 | 2500"). Good serialisation keeps each piece self-explanatory. It also affects retrieval: a header-less slice of numbers matches almost no question, while a slice with caption and headers matches questions that name the row or column. Format also affects the model's reading accuracy: well-formed Markdown tables are read much more reliably than space-aligned plain text, where columns are easily confused.`,
        e: `Each 20-row slice of a rate table becomes a chunk that starts with the caption "Travel allowance by grade and city (2026)" and the header row "| Grade | Mumbai | Pune |". Every slice can be understood on its own. Questions about any grade or city now retrieve the right slice, because every slice carries the words 'Travel allowance', 'Grade' and the city names. The repeated header costs a few tokens per slice but makes every number unambiguous.`,
        c: `
def table_chunks(caption, header, rows, size=20):
    head = "| " + " | ".join(header) + " |\\n|" + "---|" * len(header)
    for i in range(0, len(rows), size):
        body = "\\n".join("| " + " | ".join(map(str, r)) + " |" for r in rows[i:i + size])
        yield f"{caption}\\n{head}\\n{body}"          # every chunk repeats caption + header` },
      { t: `Image Captioning`,
        s: `Using a vision model to describe images in words, so they can be found by text search.`,
        h: `Each meaningful image (diagram, photo, screenshot) is sent to a vision-language model with a prompt such as "Describe this image for search; list visible labels and text". The caption is stored as text linked to the image, its page and the surrounding section, and indexed like any other chunk. Decorative images (logos, icons) are skipped. Captions can include detected text in the image (labels, numbers) for keyword search.`,
        u: `Manuals with photos and diagrams, slide decks, product catalogues, screenshots in how-to guides, and scanned forms with stamps or sketches. Caption only meaningful images: skip logos, icons and decorative photos to save cost. Store the image itself and show it next to the answer when its caption is cited, so users see what was described. Review a sample of captions per document type, because vision models can misread labels and numbers. Re-caption when a better model becomes available.`,
        p: `Information that exists only in images is invisible to text-based retrieval. Captioning makes it searchable, so questions about what a diagram or photo shows can find it. It also makes visual knowledge accessible to people using screen readers, which is a useful side benefit for internal documentation. Captions also make images rankable: once described in words, an image can be reranked and cited just like text, so the system can decide when a diagram is the best evidence.`,
        e: `A wiring photo is captioned "Red wire connects to terminal L1, blue to N, green-yellow to earth". The question "which terminal is the blue wire?" now retrieves the photo's caption and the page, and the answer cites the manual page with the photo. Captioning 800 manual images took one overnight batch, and image-related questions that used to end in 'I could not find this' started getting cited answers. Users could also open the original photo from the citation to check the wiring themselves.` },
      { t: `Chart & Diagram Understanding`,
        s: `Extracting the data or meaning from charts, graphs and diagrams.`,
        h: `A vision-language model or a chart-specialised model reads axes, legends, labels and values, turning a chart into a small data table, or describes a diagram's components and the arrows between them. The result is stored as text (table or description) linked to the original image for citation. Extracted values should be checked against any numbers in the surrounding text when available.`,
        u: `Reports and dashboards exported to PDF, investor presentations, architecture and process diagrams, scientific figures and infographics. Prefer the underlying data when it exists (a spreadsheet behind the chart); use chart reading when only the image is available. Keep the original chart image linked for verification. Store both the extracted values and a short description of the chart's main message ('revenue grew each quarter except Q4').`,
        p: `Questions like "what was Q3 revenue?" fail when the number exists only as a bar in a chart, and "what does service A depend on?" fails when the answer is only an arrow in a diagram. Converting visuals into text closes that gap. Chart reading can misread values, especially on dense or 3D charts, so extracted numbers should be presented with their source and checked for high-stakes uses. Without it, users must read charts themselves, and the assistant appears to 'not know' figures that are clearly visible in the report.`,
        e: `A bar chart in a quarterly report becomes "Revenue (₹ crore): Q1 4.1, Q2 4.6, Q3 5.2, Q4 5.0". The question "how did revenue change from Q2 to Q3?" is answered "it rose from 4.6 to 5.2 crore [1]", citing the chart's page. For a high-stakes report, the extracted numbers were compared with the appendix tables, and the two matched, which gave the team confidence in the method. Users click the citation to see the chart itself, so they can verify the values in a second.` },
      { t: `Vision-Language Page Parsing`,
        s: `Sending the image of a page to a vision LLM and asking it to write the content as Markdown.`,
        h: `Each page is rendered and sent with a prompt like "Transcribe this page as Markdown; keep headings, lists and tables". The model handles complex layouts, charts and even handwriting in one step. An alternative approach (ColPali-style) skips transcription and embeds page images directly for retrieval, then shows the page image to a vision model to answer. A quality check can compare the transcription with any native text on the page to catch hallucinated content.`,
        u: `Use it as a fallback for pages where layout parsers fail: infographics, complex forms, magazine layouts, handwritten notes, and visually rich slides. Route only difficult pages to it, because it is slow and costly. Budget it carefully: a large document sent page by page to a vision model can cost far more than classic parsing. Keep the page image and use it in citations so users can see the original. Detect difficult pages automatically, for example pages where the normal parser produced very little text or many fragments.`,
        p: `Classic parsers break on unusual layouts, producing empty or scrambled text for exactly the pages that contain important visual information. Vision-language parsing recovers them, at a higher cost per page. Without a fallback, those pages are effectively lost, and their information never reaches users. It is also the most flexible option for new or unusual document types, because it needs no layout-specific rules — but its errors are less predictable than a classic parser's, so keep checks in place.`,
        e: `A one-page benefits infographic, which Docling turned into scattered fragments, is transcribed by a vision model into clear headings and bullets ("Health insurance: ₹5 lakh family cover…"). Questions about benefits now cite that page correctly. Only 3% of pages in the library needed this fallback, so the extra cost stayed small while recovering the hardest content. Spot checks found one skipped footnote in 40 transcribed pages, which the team accepted given the gain.`,
        n: `Vision models can skip or invent text. Spot-check outputs and keep the original page image for citations.` },
      { t: `Code Block Preservation`,
        s: `Keeping source code exactly as written — indentation, line breaks, symbols — and marked as code.`,
        h: `Detect code blocks (Markdown fences, &lt;pre&gt; or &lt;code&gt; in HTML, monospace fonts in PDFs), skip unicode and whitespace "cleaning" inside them, keep each block whole when chunking (or split at function boundaries), and tag its language. Store code blocks with their surrounding explanation so they can be found by what they do. Each code block keeps a language tag such as 'bash' or 'python', which helps both rendering and retrieval. Inline code inside sentences (like a command name) is also marked so it is not altered.`,
        u: `Developer documentation, runbooks, README files, API guides, internal wikis with scripts, and configuration examples. Index the explanation around each code block together with the code, because users search by intent ('restart the payment service'), not by command syntax. Render code in the answer as a code block, never as flowing text. Code search benefits from both the exact code (keyword search) and its description (semantic search).`,
        p: `Whitespace normalisation breaks Python indentation and YAML structure; re-wrapped lines break shell commands. A code block split in half is unusable. Preserving code exactly means engineers can copy answers and run them safely. Broken code in answers is dangerous: an engineer running a mangled command during an incident can make things worse. Preserving code exactly protects against that. It also preserves the meaning of configuration: an indentation change in YAML can turn a valid configuration into an invalid or different one.`,
        e: `A runbook's bash snippet for restarting a service is returned exactly, inside a code block, so the on-call engineer can copy and run it. Before preservation, the "cleaning" step had merged its lines, turning three commands into one broken line. A test that compares code blocks before and after parsing now runs in CI, so formatting regressions are caught before release. Engineers now trust the assistant's commands enough to use it during incidents.` },
      { t: `Formula / LaTeX Extraction`,
        s: `Converting mathematical formulas in documents into LaTeX text that LLMs can read.`,
        h: `Formula-recognition models (in Docling, Nougat, Mathpix and similar tools) detect formula regions and convert them, from PDF glyphs or images, into LaTeX such as <code>\\frac{a}{b}</code> or <code>\\sum_{i} x_i</code>. The LaTeX is kept inline in the text, so chunks contain readable formulas. Inline formulas and display formulas are both handled, and equation numbers are kept so citations can refer to them.`,
        u: `Scientific and engineering papers, textbooks, finance and actuarial documents, technical standards and specifications. Render LaTeX in the answer interface so users see proper formulas. Keep the formula's equation number and page for citations. Keep a plain-language description next to complex formulas, so semantic search can find them by meaning as well as by symbols.`,
        p: `Formulas extracted as garbage symbols ("ÿ∑≠ x̂") make questions about them unanswerable and can confuse the LLM. LaTeX is a precise text representation that LLMs understand well. Formula recognition also has errors, so important formulas should be spot-checked against the original page. It also makes formulas comparable across documents, which helps questions like 'is this the same formula as in the 2024 standard?'.`,
        e: `"What is the BM25 formula?" is answered from a research paper whose equation was extracted as LaTeX, so the model can explain each term (IDF, term frequency, k1, b) correctly and cite the page. The answer cites 'equation 1, page 3', and the interface renders the LaTeX as a readable formula next to the explanation. Without LaTeX extraction, the same chunk contained unreadable symbols and the model refused to explain the formula.` },
      { t: `Slide (PPT) Parsing`,
        s: `Extracting slides in order — titles, bullet points, speaker notes, tables and images.`,
        h: `python-pptx or Docling reads each slide's shapes: the title placeholder, text boxes, tables, images and charts, plus the speaker notes (often the real explanation). Text boxes are ordered by position. Each slide is usually one chunk, with the slide number and title as its location and heading. Grouped shapes and SmartArt need special handling, because their text sits inside nested shapes.`,
        u: `Training decks, sales and strategy presentations, project updates, and onboarding materials — often the only written form of important knowledge. Index speaker notes together with slide content, because they often contain the explanation the slide only hints at. Caption important diagrams on slides, and keep slide numbers for citations. Export decks to PDF only as a last resort, because the PPTX structure (titles, notes) is richer than the PDF rendering.`,
        p: `Naive extraction returns text boxes in random order, drops speaker notes and ignores diagrams, so slide decks become unusable fragments. Slide-aware parsing keeps each slide coherent and citable. Decks are also often outdated copies of each other, so near-duplicate detection is useful for presentations. Slides are also short and fragmentary, so each slide needs its title and deck name as context to be understood on its own.`,
        e: `Slide 12 "Leave policy changes" with notes "Effective April 2026; carry-over limit reduced to 5 days" becomes one chunk cited as "HR-update.pptx, slide 12". The answer about the new carry-over limit quotes the speaker notes. Without the notes, the slide alone only said 'Carry-over changes', which was not enough to answer the question. The citation opens the deck at slide 12, where the presenter's note confirms the date.`,
        c: `
from pptx import Presentation
for n, slide in enumerate(Presentation("deck.pptx").slides, 1):
    title = slide.shapes.title.text if slide.shapes.title else ""
    body = [s.text_frame.text for s in slide.shapes if s.has_text_frame and s != slide.shapes.title]
    notes = slide.notes_slide.notes_text_frame.text if slide.has_notes_slide else ""
    print(n, title, body, notes)` },
      { t: `Audio / Video Transcription`,
        s: `Turning speech in recordings into text with timestamps.`,
        h: `A speech-to-text model such as Whisper produces text segments with start and end times; speaker diarisation can label who spoke. The transcript is chunked by time windows or topics, and each chunk keeps its timestamps, so citations can link to the exact moment in the recording. Long recordings are transcribed in segments, and a final pass can add punctuation and paragraphs.`,
        u: `Meeting recordings, all-hands talks, training videos, call-centre calls, webinars and podcasts. Check transcripts for names and domain terms, which speech models often misspell, and give the model a vocabulary hint where supported. Respect consent and privacy rules for recorded meetings. Index transcripts in time-based chunks of a few minutes, with the meeting title and date as the header.`,
        p: `Hours of recorded knowledge — decisions, explanations, announcements — are impossible to search by hand. Transcription makes them searchable and citable down to the minute. Transcripts also need cleaning: filler words, crosstalk and misheard terms add noise, so a light clean-up pass improves retrieval. Recordings are also long, so precise timestamps matter: a citation to 'minute 41' saves the user from re-watching an hour.`,
        e: `"When did the CEO talk about the new leave policy?" returns "All-hands-Oct.mp4 at 12:41", with the quoted sentence. Clicking the citation starts the video at that moment. Speaker labels also let users ask 'what did the HR head say about carry-over?' and get only that speaker's statements. Transcribing a year of all-hands meetings took one weekend of GPU time.`,
        c: `
import whisper
model = whisper.load_model("small")
for seg in model.transcribe("allhands.mp4")["segments"]:
    print(f"{seg['start']:.0f}s-{seg['end']:.0f}s: {seg['text']}")` },
      { t: `Form & Key-Value Extraction`,
        s: `Reading forms as field–value pairs, such as "Invoice number: 4471" or "Total: ₹12,400".`,
        h: `A document-AI model or rules detect labels and their values by layout (to the right of, or below, the label), including checkboxes and handwritten entries. Output is structured fields, often with confidence scores, stored as metadata or in a database table alongside the document. Low-confidence fields are flagged for human review.`,
        u: `Invoices, purchase orders, applications, claims, KYC documents, timesheets and any standard form processed in volume. Combine it with validation rules (totals add up, dates are plausible, invoice numbers follow a pattern) to catch extraction errors early. Store extracted fields in a database table as well as on the document, so both SQL and retrieval can use them.`,
        p: `Forms parsed as plain text lose which value belongs to which label, so "what is the total on invoice 4471?" may return the tax amount. Key-value extraction turns forms into reliable, filterable data. It also enables analytics and automation that text search cannot do, such as totals by vendor or detecting duplicate invoices. Manual data entry from forms is slow and error-prone; reliable extraction saves time and reduces mistakes, while confidence scores keep humans in the loop for uncertain fields.`,
        e: `From 10,000 supplier invoices, the fields Vendor, Invoice number, Date and Total are extracted. "Show invoices from Vendor X over ₹1 lakh in 2025" becomes a precise filter query, and each result cites the original invoice page. A validation rule found 23 invoices whose extracted total did not equal the sum of their line items, which revealed OCR errors on a few low-quality scans. Reviewers only check the flagged fields instead of every invoice.` },
    ]},
  ],
});
